# Copy to .env.local and fill in. Never commit .env.local.
# Anything prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_ is embedded in the browser bundle — treat it as public.

# --- Supabase (required) ---------------------------------------------------
# Project settings -> API
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# Server-only. Bypasses RLS. Never expose to the browser, never prefix NEXT_PUBLIC_.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# --- Site (optional locally) -----------------------------------------------
# Absolute origin used to build the links in confirmation emails. Defaults to
# http://localhost:3000 in development and to VERCEL_URL when deployed.
#
# Whatever this resolves to, `<origin>/auth/callback` has to be on the project's
# redirect allow list: Supabase dashboard -> Authentication -> URL Configuration
# -> Redirect URLs (http://localhost:3000/** covers local work). Without it,
# Supabase drops the confirmation code on the Site URL instead.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=


# --- Razorpay (required to assign a job) -----------------------------------
# Spec §4 charges the client at selection, so without these nobody can be
# picked. The shortlist says so rather than assigning jobs for free.
#
# Dashboard -> Account & Settings -> API Keys. Test keys (rzp_test_...) are
# free and are what local development should use.
#
# The key id is public: Razorpay Checkout needs it in the browser and it
# authorises nothing on its own. The secret never leaves the server.
NEXT_PUBLIC_RAZORPAY_KEY_ID=
RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET=

# A DIFFERENT secret from the one above — you choose it when creating the
# webhook at Dashboard -> Settings -> Webhooks. Subscribe the endpoint
# <origin>/api/webhooks/razorpay to `payment.captured` and `payment.failed`.
#
# This is the backstop for a client who paid and then closed the tab: without
# it that job is charged for and never assigned. The route refuses every
# delivery while this is unset rather than trusting an unsigned body.
RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=


# --- Worker KYC (required before anyone can be paid) -----------------------
# Spec §6 puts KYC before the first payout: full name, PAN, bank or UPI, date
# of birth, with the name checked against what the bank and the PAN registry
# return. Without these three the KYC screen says verification isn't switched
# on rather than collecting identity documents it cannot verify.

# The RazorpayX current account a penny drop is debited from — ₹1 per worker,
# one time. Dashboard -> RazorpayX -> Account Statement; it is the account
# number shown there, not anything belonging to a worker.
RAZORPAY_X_ACCOUNT_NUMBER=

# The key the stored PAN and account-number fingerprints are HMAC'd with. Any
# long random string — `openssl rand -hex 32`. It is NOT in the database, on
# purpose: the hashes exist to spot one PAN on two accounts, and a plain digest
# of a PAN is a small enough space to enumerate offline.
#
# Changing it orphans every existing hash. Duplicates stop being detectable
# across the change; nothing else breaks.
KYC_HASH_PEPPER=

# Razorpay's PAN verification endpoint, in full — https://…
#
# A URL rather than a path because this API is enabled per merchant under
# contract rather than published with the payment APIs, so the host and version
# arrive with the contract. Leave it unset and KYC still completes for workers
# under the ₹5,000 lifetime line, which spec §6 says UPI alone covers; above
# that line PAN is mandatory and the screen says so rather than storing one it
# never checked.
# RAZORPAY_PAN_VERIFY_URL=


# --- Auto-accept sweep (required for deliveries to complete on time) -------
# Spec §5: a delivery is accepted automatically after 36 hours under ₹1,000 and
# 3 days at or above it, if the client says nothing. The clock is stored on the
# delivery row; this is the bearer token on the endpoint that reads it.
#
# Any long random string — `openssl rand -hex 32`. On Vercel, set it as an
# environment variable and the schedule in vercel.json sends it automatically;
# locally, call it yourself:
#
#   curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET" http://localhost:3000/api/cron/auto-accept
#
# Without it the route refuses every request. An open endpoint that completes
# jobs and releases escrow is an open endpoint that pays people early.
CRON_SECRET=


# --- Amazon SES (transactional email) --------------------------------------
# Spec §15's essential emails. Without these the platform still queues every
# message it would have sent: development prints them to the server console and
# production leaves them in `email_outbox` as failed, with the reason on the
# row. Nothing is silently dropped — silence is the failure mode spec §15 is
# actually warning about.
#
# Named SES_* rather than AWS_* on purpose. A Vercel function runs on Lambda,
# whose runtime sets AWS_REGION / AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID / AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY for
# its own execution role — ours would be shadowed in some contexts and not
# others. The credentials should also belong to an IAM user holding
# `ses:SendEmail` and nothing else.
#
# SES identities are regional: the region here must be the one the domain was
# verified in. ap-south-1 for a platform whose users are all in India.
#
# Full setup — SPF, DKIM, the custom MAIL FROM and leaving the sandbox — is in
# docs/email-setup.md. Start with:  node scripts/ses-domain.ts setup zevi.in
SES_REGION=
SES_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
SES_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# The From address, which must be a verified identity — the address itself or
# its domain. The display-name form is accepted: `Zevi <no-reply@zevi.in>`.
SES_FROM_ADDRESS=

# Optional. Where replies go if not to the From address.
# SES_REPLY_TO_ADDRESS=

# The configuration set SES publishes send events through, and the SNS topic it
# publishes them to. Required for bounce and complaint handling, which is not
# optional for long: SES suspends an account over a 5% bounce rate or a 0.1%
# complaint rate, and both are measured across the whole account — so a hundred
# dead addresses take the receipts and payout notices down with them.
#
# Subscribe the topic to <origin>/api/webhooks/ses. That endpoint refuses every
# delivery while the ARN is unset, because an SNS signature proves Amazon sent
# the message and not that it came from *our* topic — anyone with an AWS account
# can point one here. Setup is docs/email-setup.md, and the SES half of it is:
#
#   node scripts/ses-domain.ts notifications zevi-email arn:aws:sns:...
SES_CONFIGURATION_SET=
SES_SNS_TOPIC_ARN=


# --- MSG91 (mobile OTP at signup) ------------------------------------------
# Spec §5 and §6 both make mobile verification compulsory, and resolveStep()
# will not move a user past it — so without these nobody finishes signing up on
# a production deployment. Leave them unset locally: development prints the code
# to the server console and the flow works end to end.
#
# All three or none. India's DLT regime checks the entity, the header and the
# template together, so two out of three is indistinguishable from zero.
#
# The message text is NOT in this codebase. DLT registers the wording with the
# operators before anything is sent, so what the app sends is a template id and
# a set of variables (OTP, VALIDITY). Registration takes 3-7 working days and is
# the whole of docs/sms-setup.md — read it before filling these in.

# MSG91 -> Settings -> API Keys. One key, full account access: treat it like
# RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET, not like an SES key scoped to one action.
MSG91_AUTH_KEY=

# The DLT-registered header the message arrives from — six alphanumeric
# characters, e.g. ZEVIIN. Must be registered TRANSACTIONAL, not promotional:
# promotional headers are blocked on DND numbers, which is most of India.
MSG91_SENDER_ID=

# MSG91 -> Templates. The id of the approved template, a long hex string. Its
# text must match what DLT approved character for character — a mismatch is
# accepted by the API, billed, and dropped by the operator.
MSG91_OTP_TEMPLATE_ID=


# --- Not wired up yet ------------------------------------------------------
# Listed so the full set is discoverable. Add to src/lib/env.ts when each
# integration actually lands.

# Cloudflare R2 (file storage)
# R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
# R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# R2_BUCKET=
# R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=

# Razorpay Route transfers (splitting a payment at capture). Needs a *linked
# account* per worker, which is Razorpay's sub-merchant onboarding and a
# separate thing from the contact and fund account KYC now creates. Escrow is
# held on the platform account and recorded in escrow_holds meanwhile.

# Sentry (error monitoring)
# SENTRY_DSN=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=
